318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425318} :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425331}] :question/math-academy-id "q-333485" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Eigenvalues" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050967 :topic/math-academy-id 3240 :topic/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Repeated Eigenvalues"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164382} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164383}] :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164385} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164386}] :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164384}] :question/math-academy-id "q-332768" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + 4y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) - 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $λ =-1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system. The equilibrium point (origin) is a\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnode."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.ty